@* TextField's Overview, written by hand, after Button's: a sentence and an example per section,
   code alone where a preview would show nothing new (types change the keyboard, not the look).
   Mode support, accessibility and the parameters follow; the API page has the full reference. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A text field is a field for one line of text, such as a name, an email address or a password. It suits short text. It's a native input, so it posts with the form, <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too. For several lines, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/text-area">TextArea</Anchor>. For numbers, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/number-field">NumberField</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and hints</Heading>
<TextBlock>A field's label comes from the model: its <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>, or the property name in words. <Code>[Required]</Code> adds a required mark. To replace the label, set <Code>Label</Code>; to add help under the field, set <Code>Hint</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-labels" />

<Heading Level="2">Types</Heading>
<TextBlock>A field's type sets the on-screen keyboard, and for email and URL, the browser's check. To make the field an email, phone, URL, search or password field, set <Code>Type</Code>. The type defaults from <Code>[EmailAddress]</Code>, <Code>[Phone]</Code>, <Code>[Url]</Code> and <Code>[DataType(DataType.Password)]</Code>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Types</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Passwords</Heading>
<TextBlock>Once IzzyBlazor's script runs, a password field gets a button that shows and hides the password. To leave the button out, set <Code>Reveal="false"</Code>. While the user types with Caps Lock on, "Caps Lock is on." shows under the field.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-password" />

<Heading Level="2">A new password's requirements</Heading>
<TextBlock>A new password field can list the rules a password must meet, and check off each one as the user types. Set <Code>Requirements</Code> to the rules your server checks. The browser doesn't post the form until the password meets all of them, and the server checks them again, so a form post with JavaScript off gets the same check. <Code>AutoComplete</Code> becomes <Code>new-password</Code>, and Safari suggests passwords that meet the rules.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-new-password" />
<TextBlock><Code>PasswordRules</Code> uses the names of ASP.NET Core Identity's <Code>PasswordOptions</Code>, and counts characters as Identity does: <Code>RequiredLength</Code>, <Code>RequireDigit</Code>, <Code>RequireLowercase</Code>, <Code>RequireUppercase</Code>, <Code>RequireNonAlphanumeric</Code> and <Code>RequiredUniqueChars</Code>. Its rules are off until set, where Identity's defaults are on, so copy each of Identity's values across for the list and the server to agree.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Anchor Href="https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html">NIST SP 800-63B</Anchor> asks for a length of at least 15 characters, or 8 where a second factor is also needed, and says not to impose other composition rules, such as "a number". The other rules are here because many existing apps have them. NIST also asks for a check against commonly used and breached passwords. Your server makes that check when it saves the password, such as with <Anchor Href="https://haveibeenpwned.com/API/v3#PwnedPasswords">Pwned Passwords</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Icons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A field can show an icon inside it, such as a search icon. Set <Code>StartIcon</Code> or <Code>EndIcon</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-icons" />

<Heading Level="2">A clear button</Heading>
<TextBlock>A clear button empties the field. It shows while the field has a value. Set <Code>Clearable</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-clearable" />
<TextBlock>Every field whose value can be empty has <Code>Clearable</Code>. A <Code>Select</Code> clears back to its <Code>Placeholder</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Add-ons</Heading>
<TextBlock>Add-ons attach text, a <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor> or a button to the side of the field. Set <Code>StartAddon</Code> or <Code>EndAddon</Code>. A submit button in an add-on posts the form, so a search box works with JavaScript off.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-addons" />

<Heading Level="2">Validation</Heading>
<TextBlock>A field's rules come from the model's attributes. Before the form post, the browser checks required, length, range, email and URL, with your <Code>ErrorMessage</Code>. After it, the server checks every rule and shows its messages under the field.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-validation" />

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, read-only and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the field up with buttons and other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-field-states" />
<TextBlock>Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>A password field's reveal button has a name for screen readers. To set it for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>ShowPasswordLabel</Code></td><td>The name of the button that shows and hides a password</td><td>"Show password"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<TextBlock>The Caps Lock warning and the requirements come from the app's text:</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>IzzyText</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>CapsLockOn</Code></td><td>The warning under a password field</td><td>"Caps Lock is on."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordLength</Code>, <Code>PasswordUnique</Code></td><td>The length and different-characters rules; <Code>{0}</Code> is the number</td><td>"At least {0} characters", "At least {0} different characters"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordLowercase</Code>, <Code>PasswordUppercase</Code>, <Code>PasswordDigit</Code>, <Code>PasswordSymbol</Code></td><td>The other rules</td><td>"A lowercase letter", "An uppercase letter", "A number", "A symbol, such as ! or #"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>RuleMet</Code>, <Code>RuleNotMet</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear after each rule</td><td>"met", "not yet"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordRulesMet</Code></td><td>Announced once all the rules are met</td><td>"All requirements met."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordRulesUnmet</Code></td><td>The error for a password that misses one; <Code>{0}</Code> is the label</td><td>"The {0} field doesn't meet every requirement."</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

